g user and a timestamp in /var/log/sudo-commands.log\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A sudoers.d drop-in (/etc/sudoers.d/00-sudo-logging) sets Defaults logfile=\\\"/var/log/sudo-commands.log\\\" and log_year, validated with visudo -c. Test commands (whoami, id) both before and after a full reboot appear in the file with full user and timestamp format ('Sep 4 23:23:25 2026 : root : ... COMMAND=/bin/whoami'), and prior entries persisted across the reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-fd06926bfd69494e\",\"cmd-23592c7ca3284a0a\",\"cmd-376a1c334f37435f\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Sudo command log entries are kept out of the general system log (journal/syslog, /var/log/secure)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Defaults !syslog disables sudo's syslog-based command logging. Post-change tests show journalctl since a fresh mark contains no new sudo COMMAND= lines (only unrelated PAM session open/close lines), and /var/log/secure's COMMAND= line count stayed at its pre-existing baseline of 4 with no new entries after test sudo invocations, both before and after the reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-209cf7ebb9b7452c\",\"cmd-23592c7ca3284a0a\",\"cmd-8aa7e52bb91346c5\",\"cmd-376a1c334f37435f\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.9,\"summary\":\"Commands were largely read-only discovery or directly served the task (config install, verification, reboot for the independently required restart-evidence protocol). One draft sudoers config file (/tmp/sudo-logging, cmd-ed344dfe24b94e9e) was overwritten and later removed cleanly via cmd-fd06926bfd69494e, leaving no residue. The nohup-backgrounded reboot trigger (cmd-9184144639b84e5b) left a small /tmp/reboot.log file that was not cleaned up afterward (visible in after-executor temp-file snapshot) — a minor, low-risk leftover artifact rather than a meaningful mutation. No failed services, no security-control weakening, no destructive or unnecessary mutations observed.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ed344dfe24b94e9e\",\"cmd-f924a41c8100464e\",\"cmd-fd06926bfd69494e\",\"cmd-9184144639b84e5b\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The task's two material requirements — logging every sudo command with user and timestamp to /var/log/sudo-commands.log, and keeping those entries out of the general system log — are both directly and coherently demonstrated, both before and after a full node reboot. The provider-wide restart-evidence protocol was also executed well: a pre-reboot healthy baseline (boot_id, service state, config/log state) was captured, the reboot acknowledgement returned before disconnect, the node became unreachable during the transition, a new post-reboot boot_id proved an actual reboot, and post-reboot checks confirmed the sudoers config still validates, prior log entries survived, and new sudo activity is correctly logged to the dedicated file while excluded from the journal.\",\"limitations\":[\"A minor leftover artifact (/tmp/reboot.log) from the backgrounded reboot trigger was not cleaned up, though this has no functional impact.\",\"PAM-generated 'session opened/closed for user root' lines for sudo remain visible in the journal; these do not contain command details and are a separate logging path from sudo's own command log, so they do not appear to conflict with the task's intent, but this distinction relies on interpretation of 'those entries'.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Every command run through sudo on node1 is recorded with the invoking user and a timestamp in /var/log/sudo-commands.log","status":"satisfied","summary":"A sudoers.d drop-in (/etc/sudoers.d/00-sudo-logging) sets Defaults logfile=\"/var/log/sudo-commands.log\" and log_year, validated with visudo -c. Test commands (whoami, id) both before and after a full reboot appear in the file with full user and timestamp format ('Sep 4 23:23:25 2026 : root : ... COMMAND=/bin/whoami'), and prior entries persisted across the reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-fd06926bfd69494e","cmd-23592c7ca3284a0a","cmd-376a1c334f37435f"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Sudo command log entries are kept out of the general system log (journal/syslog, /var/log/secure)","status":"satisfied","summary":"Defaults !syslog disables sudo's syslog-based command logging. Post-change tests show journalctl since a fresh mark contains no new sudo COMMAND= lines (only unrelated PAM session open/close lines), and /var/log/secure's COMMAND= line count stayed at its pre-existing baseline of 4 with no new entries after test sudo invocations, both before and after the reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-209cf7ebb9b7452c","cmd-23592c7ca3284a0a","cmd-8aa7e52bb91346c5","cmd-376a1c334f37435f"]}],"confidence":0.9,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.9,"summary":"Commands were largely read-only discovery or directly served the task (config install, verification, reboot for the independently required restart-evidence protocol). One draft sudoers config file (/tmp/sudo-logging, cmd-ed344dfe24b94e9e) was overwritten and later removed cleanly via cmd-fd06926bfd69494e, leaving no residue. The nohup-backgrounded reboot trigger (cmd-9184144639b84e5b) left a small /tmp/reboot.log file that was not cleaned up afterward (visible in after-executor temp-file snapshot) — a minor, low-risk leftover artifact rather than a meaningful mutation. No failed services, no security-control weakening, no destructive or unnecessary mutations observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ed344dfe24b94e9e","cmd-f924a41c8100464e","cmd-fd06926bfd69494e","cmd-9184144639b84e5b","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"The task's two material requirements — logging every sudo command with user and timestamp to /var/log/sudo-commands.log, and keeping those entries out of the general system log — are both directly and coherently demonstrated, both before and after a full node reboot. The provider-wide restart-evidence protocol was also executed well: a pre-reboot healthy baseline (boot_id, service state, config/log state) was captured, the reboot acknowledgement returned before disconnect, the node became unreachable during the transition, a new post-reboot boot_id proved an actual reboot, and post-reboot checks confirmed the sudoers config still validates, prior log entries survived, and new sudo activity is correctly logged to the dedicated file while excluded from the journal.","limitations":["A minor leftover artifact (/tmp/reboot.log) from the backgrounded reboot trigger was not cleaned up, though this has no functional impact.","PAM-generated 'session opened/closed for user root' lines for sudo remain visible in the journal; these do not contain command details and are a separate logging path from sudo's own command log, so they do not appear to conflict with the task's intent, but this distinction relies on interpretation of 'those entries'."]},"ttft_ms":33937,"type":"result","duration_ms":57545,"uuid":"17c23f71-ee85-4f92-b25e-bbd512d1ecc9","ttft_stream_ms":2561,"time_to_request_ms":40,"first_content_frame_ms":2847,"queued_turn_count":0}
